<title>USA: USDA AUG 12 U.S. CROP HIGHLIGHTS -- USDA.</title>
<headline>USDA AUG 12 U.S. CROP HIGHLIGHTS -- USDA.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-08-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>The U.S. Agriculture Department Tuesday forecast the 1997 U.S. soybean crop at a record 2.744 billion bushels, up 15 percent from 1996.</p>
<p>In its monthly Crop Production report, USDA also forecast the U.S. corn and wheat crops to rise from year-ago levels. The U.S. all wheat yield was estimated at a record high of 39.9 bushels per acre, up 1.6 bushels per acre from last month.</p>
<p>However, it said the U.S. cotton crop would be down six percent from 1996 at 17.78 million bales.  </p>
<p>High prices this past spring encouraged farmers to plant the most soybeans since 1982. The increased production will alleviate some of the current tightness in soybean supplies.</p>
<p>USDA forecasts soybean stocks on hand at the end of the 1997/98 marketing year to total 305 million bushels, up from its estimate of 125 million for 1996/97.  </p>
